1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,309
A pump fills a water tank.

2
00:00:02,309 --> 00:00:03,758
A crane lifts steel.

3
00:00:03,758 --> 00:00:05,812
You climb the stairs to class.

4
00:00:06,212 --> 00:00:07,465
All three do work.

5
00:00:07,465 --> 00:00:08,952
But some do it faster.

6
00:00:08,952 --> 00:00:11,323
What do we call that speed of working?

7
00:00:11,773 --> 00:00:15,472
Aarav and Diya race up the same
college stairs, to the same floor.

8
00:00:15,822 --> 00:00:17,585
Diya gets there in ten seconds.

9
00:00:17,585 --> 00:00:18,507
Aarav in five.

10
00:00:18,507 --> 00:00:19,724
They weigh the same.

11
00:00:19,724 --> 00:00:20,858
Who did more work?

12
00:00:21,358 --> 00:00:25,411
By the end, you will know who did
more work, and who had more power.

13
00:00:26,061 --> 00:00:27,349
So what is power?

14
00:00:27,699 --> 00:00:29,830
Work is a force moving something.

15
00:00:29,830 --> 00:00:32,404
Lifting your body up the stairs is work.

16
00:00:32,804 --> 00:00:35,160
Power is how fast that work is done.

17
00:00:35,160 --> 00:00:37,841
The work done, divided by the time taken.

18
00:00:38,241 --> 00:00:40,628
Same work in less time means more power.

19
00:00:41,228 --> 00:00:42,838
Its unit is the watt.

20
00:00:42,838 --> 00:00:45,796
One watt is one joule
of work every second.

21
00:00:46,446 --> 00:00:48,892
Here are the stairs, three metres high.

22
00:00:48,892 --> 00:00:50,546
Both weigh fifty kilograms.

23
00:00:50,896 --> 00:00:55,037
Lifting fifty kilograms up three
metres takes m g h of work.

24
00:00:55,037 --> 00:00:58,031
That is fourteen hundred
and seventy joules.

25
00:00:58,431 --> 00:01:00,765
Aarav reaches the top in five seconds.

26
00:01:00,765 --> 00:01:01,836
Diya takes ten.

27
00:01:02,236 --> 00:01:03,486
Same work for both.

28
00:01:03,486 --> 00:01:06,343
But Aarav's power is two
hundred ninety four watts.

29
00:01:06,343 --> 00:01:08,252
Diya's, one hundred forty seven.

30
00:01:08,652 --> 00:01:10,810
On the graph, work against time.

31
00:01:10,810 --> 00:01:13,023
Both lines end at the same height.

32
00:01:13,023 --> 00:01:15,377
The steeper line is the bigger power.

33
00:01:15,877 --> 00:01:17,946
Slow-mo replay, halfway up.

34
00:01:18,296 --> 00:01:22,489
He pushes up with his weight, four
ninety newtons, rising at zero point

35
00:01:22,489 --> 00:01:23,944
six metres per second.

36
00:01:23,944 --> 00:01:24,844
Multiply.

37
00:01:24,640 --> 00:01:26,425
Two ninety four watts again.

38
00:01:27,075 --> 00:01:30,896
Let's see why force times speed
gives power, step by step.

39
00:01:31,246 --> 00:01:32,156
Step one.

40
00:01:32,156 --> 00:01:36,548
Average power is the total
work, divided by the total time.

41
00:01:36,548 --> 00:01:38,156
P equals W over t.

42
00:01:38,906 --> 00:01:39,852
Step two.

43
00:01:39,852 --> 00:01:43,082
For one instant, P equals d W by d t.

44
00:01:43,082 --> 00:01:46,244
A tiny bit of work, over a tiny time.

45
00:01:46,994 --> 00:01:47,934
Step three.

46
00:01:47,934 --> 00:01:51,521
A tiny bit of work is force
dot a tiny displacement.

47
00:01:51,521 --> 00:01:53,872
So P equals F dot d r by d t.

48
00:01:54,622 --> 00:01:55,663
Step four.

49
00:01:55,663 --> 00:01:58,152
d r by d t is the velocity.

50
00:01:58,152 --> 00:02:02,064
So P equals F dot v,
which is F v cos theta.

51
00:02:02,814 --> 00:02:03,714
Units.

52
00:02:03,293 --> 00:02:05,996
Newtons times metres per
second is joules per second.

53
00:02:05,996 --> 00:02:07,049
That is the watt.

54
00:02:07,749 --> 00:02:08,829
Remember this.

55
00:02:08,829 --> 00:02:11,070
Power is how fast work is done.

56
00:02:11,070 --> 00:02:12,484
P equals F dot v.

57
00:02:13,184 --> 00:02:15,514
Now, work and power, side by side.

58
00:02:15,864 --> 00:02:17,310
Work asks how much.

59
00:02:17,310 --> 00:02:19,470
Diya lifts herself three metres.

60
00:02:19,470 --> 00:02:21,107
Fourteen seventy joules.

61
00:02:21,507 --> 00:02:22,946
Power asks how fast.

62
00:02:22,946 --> 00:02:26,772
Aarav does the same fourteen
seventy joules in half the time.

63
00:02:26,772 --> 00:02:28,009
Double the power.

64
00:02:28,609 --> 00:02:29,607
Now you try.

65
00:02:29,607 --> 00:02:32,803
Two cranes lift the same
load to the same height.

66
00:02:32,803 --> 00:02:34,353
One is twice as slow.

67
00:02:34,353 --> 00:02:35,903
Does it do less work?

68
00:02:35,903 --> 00:02:37,143
Pause and decide.

69
00:02:40,343 --> 00:02:41,243
No!

70
00:02:40,790 --> 00:02:42,960
The work is the same, m g h.

71
00:02:42,960 --> 00:02:46,160
It only has less power,
because it takes longer.

72
00:02:46,810 --> 00:02:47,710
One more.

73
00:02:47,596 --> 00:02:52,052
A crane lifts a load at a steady
speed, now twice as fast as before.

74
00:02:52,052 --> 00:02:53,825
What happens to its power?

75
00:02:53,825 --> 00:02:55,052
Pause and decide.

76
00:02:58,252 --> 00:02:59,152
It doubles!

77
00:02:59,129 --> 00:03:02,561
The force is still the
weight, and the speed doubles.

78
00:03:02,561 --> 00:03:03,648
P equals F v.

79
00:03:04,298 --> 00:03:07,006
So why does Aarav cycle
slowly up a steep hill?

80
00:03:07,006 --> 00:03:08,090
Follow the links.

81
00:03:08,440 --> 00:03:09,340
One.

82
00:03:08,930 --> 00:03:11,414
His legs can give only so much power.

83
00:03:11,414 --> 00:03:12,848
That limit is fixed.

84
00:03:13,248 --> 00:03:14,148
Two.

85
00:03:13,713 --> 00:03:15,628
Power equals force times speed.

86
00:03:16,028 --> 00:03:16,928
Three.

87
00:03:16,550 --> 00:03:20,083
A steep hill needs a bigger
force, to push him up the slope.

88
00:03:20,483 --> 00:03:21,383
Four.

89
00:03:21,034 --> 00:03:24,677
With power fixed, a bigger
force means a smaller speed.

90
00:03:24,677 --> 00:03:25,997
So he slows down.

91
00:03:26,597 --> 00:03:28,287
Now, one worked example.

92
00:03:28,637 --> 00:03:32,736
A lift and its passengers, eight
hundred kilograms, go up at a steady one

93
00:03:32,736 --> 00:03:34,465
point five metres per second.

94
00:03:34,465 --> 00:03:36,367
Friction is one thousand newtons.

95
00:03:36,867 --> 00:03:40,021
The motor must hold up the
weight and beat friction.

96
00:03:40,021 --> 00:03:41,996
Force equals m g plus friction.

97
00:03:41,996 --> 00:03:44,290
Seventy eight forty plus one thousand.

98
00:03:44,290 --> 00:03:45,973
Eighty eight forty newtons.

99
00:03:46,723 --> 00:03:48,817
Power equals force times speed.

100
00:03:48,817 --> 00:03:51,480
Eighty eight forty times one point five.

101
00:03:51,480 --> 00:03:54,183
Thirteen thousand two hundred sixty watts.

102
00:03:54,933 --> 00:03:57,281
About thirteen point three kilowatts.

103
00:03:57,281 --> 00:04:01,497
At seven hundred forty six watts per
horsepower, about seventeen point

104
00:04:01,497 --> 00:04:02,691
eight horsepower.

105
00:04:03,341 --> 00:04:04,802
Now, the exam trap.

106
00:04:05,152 --> 00:04:09,227
Many students say a kilowatt hour is a
unit of power, because it has kilowatt

107
00:04:09,227 --> 00:04:10,127
in it.

108
00:04:10,135 --> 00:04:12,202
But a kilowatt hour is energy.

109
00:04:12,202 --> 00:04:14,015
One kilowatt for one hour.

110
00:04:14,015 --> 00:04:16,582
That is three point six million joules.

111
00:04:17,232 --> 00:04:18,607
Where is this asked?

112
00:04:18,607 --> 00:04:22,742
In numericals on lifts, pumps and
motors, and power from F dot v.

113
00:04:23,342 --> 00:04:24,565
Exam level now.

114
00:04:24,565 --> 00:04:29,634
A thousand kilogram car climbs a slope
at a steady twenty metres per second.

115
00:04:29,634 --> 00:04:31,656
Sine theta is one twentieth.

116
00:04:31,656 --> 00:04:35,118
Resistance is five hundred
newtons, and g is ten.

117
00:04:35,118 --> 00:04:36,766
Find the engine power.

118
00:04:36,766 --> 00:04:38,041
Pause and solve.

119
00:04:43,441 --> 00:04:44,895
The answer is B.

120
00:04:44,895 --> 00:04:46,270
Twenty kilowatts.

121
00:04:46,770 --> 00:04:51,088
Steady speed, so the engine force
balances everything pulling back.

122
00:04:51,588 --> 00:04:55,483
Gravity along the slope is m g
sine theta, five hundred newtons.

123
00:04:55,483 --> 00:04:56,741
Add the resistance.

124
00:04:56,741 --> 00:04:58,091
One thousand newtons.

125
00:04:58,591 --> 00:05:00,056
Power equals F v.

126
00:05:00,056 --> 00:05:02,013
One thousand times twenty.

127
00:05:02,013 --> 00:05:04,910
Twenty thousand watts, twenty kilowatts.

128
00:05:05,510 --> 00:05:07,050
Here is your recap.

129
00:05:07,050 --> 00:05:08,632
Screenshot this card.

130
00:05:11,832 --> 00:05:14,018
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

131
00:05:14,418 --> 00:05:15,318
One.

132
00:05:14,878 --> 00:05:18,178
Who does more work, the fast
climber or the slow one?

133
00:05:19,078 --> 00:05:19,978
Two.

134
00:05:19,556 --> 00:05:22,151
Why does power equal force times velocity?

135
00:05:23,051 --> 00:05:23,951
Three.

136
00:05:23,589 --> 00:05:26,323
Is a kilowatt hour a
unit of power or energy?

137
00:05:27,423 --> 00:05:31,730
Next time: a gas flame heats a
pan, lights the kitchen and hisses.

138
00:05:31,730 --> 00:05:33,735
Where does all that energy go?

139
00:05:33,735 --> 00:05:35,846
We find out in forms of energy.
